// =============================================================================
// MemQL Automation Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `automation` syntax. It is NOT loaded
// by the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to
// skip it -- see component/memql/dslfs/walker.go).
//
// `automation` is the behavioral DSL's ONLY reactive + composing construct.
// From the behavioral-constructs ADR
// (docs/internal/design/dsl-behavioral-constructs-adr.md, §2):
//
//     logic decides, mutations persist, actions touch the world,
//     queries read, automations orchestrate and react.
//
// An automation is triggered by an event / schedule / CDC change (or invoked
// by reference, ADR §2.2) and sequences steps. It is the one place the
// decide -> persist -> act rhythm comes together: call `logic` to decide,
// call a `mutation` to persist one write, call an `action` to reach the
// outside world, call a `query` to read, and call a sub-automation to
// compose. It is also the only construct that may carry a `@trigger`.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Call-graph contract (ADR §2 table) -- enforced by the I2 call-graph validator
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Construct  | May call                                       | May NOT
//   -----------|------------------------------------------------|---------
//   automation | logic, query, mutation, action, sub-automation;| --
//              | branch (switch / success-failure), loop        |
//              | (forEach), fan-out (parallel)                  |
//
// Automations consume the layers above them and never the other way around:
// a logic/query/mutation/action never calls back into an automation.
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. Automation-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
//   @enabled / @disabled    Lifecycle flag. @enabled is the explicit-on
//                           default; @disabled skips the automation at load.
//   @description("text")    Human-readable description.            optional
//   @trigger(...)           The reactive surface. EXACTLY ONE per automation
//                           (the only construct allowed to carry it).
//
// Trigger forms:
//   @trigger(event="<name>", concept="v1:<ns>:<concept>", partition="*")
//                           Fire on a graph/event-bus event. `concept` scopes
//                           it to a concept; `partition="*"` is still required
//                           while the event topic carries a partition segment
//                           (#56 phase 8 removes it).
//   @trigger(schedule="<6-field cron>")
//                           Time-driven (sec min hour dom mon dow), e.g.
//                           @trigger(schedule="0 */10 * * * *") = every 10 min.
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. Terse single-step form -- replaces the pass-through ceremony (ADR §2.4)
// =============================================================================
//
// Most automations existed only to make a `logic` reactive: their whole body
// was `step run { logic X(event: event) }`. The terse form expresses that
// in one line WITHOUT hiding the reactive surface (it stays greppable):
//
//     automation NAME @trigger(event="...") => logic logicName
//
// The loader lowers it to the canonical single-step automation; it forwards
// the trigger payload as `event` when the target logic declares args, and
// calls it with an empty object otherwise. The terse and longhand forms
// compile to IDENTICAL runtime automations (dry-run + live parity).
//
// Examples (the cluster pass-throughs, terse):
//
//     automation registerNode   @trigger(event="system.startup")  => logic registerNode
//     automation deregisterNode  @trigger(event="system.shutdown") => logic deregisterNode
//     automation pruneStaleNodes @trigger(schedule="0 */10 * * * *") => logic pruneStaleClusterNodes
//
// NOTE: terse-form parsing lands in I3 (#2215); this section documents the
// committed target syntax (ADR §7) ahead of that parser work.
//
// =============================================================================
//   3. Full form -- the canonical longhand single-step automation
// =============================================================================
//
// The longhand the terse form lowers to. `event` is the bound trigger
// payload; `args.X` reaches its fields inside step calls.

@enabled
@trigger(event="system.startup")
@description("Register this node in the database on startup (longhand single-step form).")
automation registerNodeLonghand {
  step run {
    logic registerNode(event: event)
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   3a. Typed input contract -- the `args { }` block  (AVAILABLE, G1 #2363)
// =============================================================================
//
// A full-form automation MAY declare a typed input contract with an
// `args { }` block, exactly like logic/query (event-payload-binding ADR
// Decision 1). It replaces the ~60-line payload COMMENT block that emitters
// and consumers used to hand-maintain with a machine-checked schema.
//
// Field grammar (identical to logic/query args):
//   <name> <type> [@required] [@enum("a","b")] [@description("...")]
//                 [@maxLength(N)] [@pattern("re")]
//   -- `@default` is REJECTED on an args field (#991); default in the body
//      via `coalesce(args.X, <v>)`.
//
// Fire-time validation (ADR Decision 2): when the automation declares an
// args block, the trigger path binds `event.payload` -> `args` and validates
// it BEFORE any step (and before the `@trigger @filter(...)`):
//   - a missing @required field / type mismatch / @enum miss / @maxLength
//     overrun / @pattern miss makes the automation REFUSE to fire -- a loud
//     Warn (automation / topic / field / reason) + a skip counter. Never a
//     silent nil, never a partial run.
//   - unknown extra payload fields are TOLERATED (tolerant-reader): not
//     bound, Debug-noted.
// The invoke-by-reference path validates its params against the SAME contract.
//
// Reading the fields inside the body:
//   - `args.<field>` -- the validated map is exposed as `args` (G1).
//   - bare `<field>` -- resolves loop-var > step name > args field; a typo'd
//     or shadowed name is a LOAD ERROR, never a silent nil (G2, #2364).
//   - punned `f(environment)` == `f(environment: environment)` -- a bare
//     simple identifier in a construct-call arg position (G3, #2365); any
//     other positional argument on a construct call is rejected.
// Envelope intrinsics stay prefixed: `event.topic`, `event.kind`,
// `event.timestamp` (RFC3339 occurrence time; distinct from `now`), and
// `event.actor.id` (the acting identity, present when the emitter stamped
// it -- graph CDC events do; check with exists(event.actor)).

@enabled
@trigger(event="deploy.requested", concept="v1:cluster:deployment", partition="*")
@description("Full form with a typed args contract (G1).")
automation deployWithArgs {
  args {
    environment     string   @required @enum("development", "staging", "production")
    engineNodeTypes []string @required
    workdir         string   @required
  }
  step gate {
    logic requireForwardDeploy(environment: args.environment, workdir: args.workdir)
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   4. Full form -- multi-step orchestration (decide / persist / act / branch)
// =============================================================================
//
// The deployment bundle's shape (illustrative; the real bundle lands in
// Phase 3). Engine mesh ONLY -- no bff/copresent. Actions default to the
// `cockpit/runner` surface; the env `switch` stays in ONE automation.
//
// NOTE (G5, #2367): `event.payload.X` reads are RETIRED in automation
// bodies -- declare the field in the `args { }` block and read it bare
// (or as `args.X`); the migrator is scripts/migrations/event_payload_args.
// `event.actor.id` is the envelope actor (G4, #2366): populated on graph CDC
// events (.created / .updated) from the mutation's acting identity; absent
// when an emitter carries no actor -- guard with exists(event.actor).
//
// CONDITION VOCABULARY (P4, #2371 -- enforced by the callgraph validator +
// the whole-tree contract test + the authoring sandbox at define time).
// A condition (if gate / forEach where / @filter) may use:
//   - step-result comparisons: steps.<decide>.result == <value>
//   - presence guards: exists(<ref>)
//   - SINGLE-value fan-out equality: <field> == "<one literal>"
//   - boolean fields / == true / == false
// FINDINGS (policy in orchestration -- move it to a pure decide logic or a
// query pushdown):
//   - a same-field string-literal ||-vocabulary (x == "a" || x == "b")
//   - date math / defaults in conditions (addDuration / coalesce / concat)
// Switch SUBJECTS are exempt: switching on a decided value IS the sanctioned
// fan-out shape.
//
// Step vocabulary an automation may use:
//   step NAME { logic L(...) }              -- decide (pure)
//   step NAME { query Q(...) }              -- read
//   step NAME { mutation M(...) }           -- persist (one write per step)
//   step NAME { action act(...) on surface(<expr>) }
//                                          -- touch the world (external capability)
//   step NAME { automation Sub(...) }       -- compose a sub-automation
//   forEach x in <list> { ... }            -- loop / fan one step over a list
//   switch <expr> { case "v" { ... } default { ... } }   -- branch
//   parallel { ... }                       -- fan-out concurrently
//   on success when <logic> { ... } / on failure { ... }  -- terminal branch
//
// A `forEach` source may be a collection chain from the in-memory
// collection/lambda library (ADR §2.2, #2302) -- e.g.
// `forEach m in args.members.where(m => m.active) { ... }`. Lambda bodies stay
// PURE (no mutations / actions inside the lambda); the side effects live in
// the forEach step body. See `_logic.memql` §6 for the full method surface.
//
//   @enabled
//   @trigger(event="deploy.requested", concept="v1:cluster:deployment")
//   @description("Deploy the memQL ENGINE cluster to an environment. No bff/copresent.")
//   automation deployEngineCluster {
//     step authorize { logic requireForwardDeploy(event: event) }   // requiresDeveloperOrAbove
//     step record    { mutation createDeployment(                   // ONE write
//                        deploymentId,          // punned (G3)
//                        environment,           // punned (G3)
//                        triggeredBy:  event.actor.id) }
//     step clone     { action cloneRepoAtVersion(
//                        workdir, ref)          // punned (G3)
//                      on surface("cockpit/runner") }
//     step build     { forEach nodeType in logic engineNodeTypes() {
//                        action buildEngineImage(
//                          nodeType, version, workdir) } }  // punned
//     step place {
//       switch environment {
//         case "development" {
//           forEach nodeType in logic engineNodeTypes() {
//             action importImageToK3d(nodeType: nodeType, cluster: "memql") }
//         }
//         default {  // staging | production -> GitOps (deploys = merges)
//           action pinOverlayDigests(
//             overlayPath: logic overlayPathForEnv(env: environment),
//             digests: $steps.build.result)
//           action argoSync(app: "memql", revision: ref)
//         }
//       }
//     }
//     step gate { action runDeployGate(
//                   env: environment, workdir) }
//     step finish {
//       on success when logic deployGatePassed(gate: $steps.gate.result) {
//         mutation updateDeploymentStatus(deploymentId,          // punned (G3) status: "succeeded")
//         action notifyDeploy(env: environment, status: "succeeded")
//       }
//       on failure {
//         action revertOverlay(overlayPath: logic overlayPathForEnv(env: environment))
//         mutation updateDeploymentStatus(deploymentId,          // punned (G3) status: "failed")
//         action notifyDeploy(env: environment, status: "failed")
//       }
//     }
//   }
//
// =============================================================================
//   5. NON-COMPLIANT examples
// =============================================================================
//
// 5a. Two writes in one step. Each mutation step performs exactly one graph
//     write; split a second write into its own step.
//
//   // INVALID: two mutations crammed into one step.
//   step record {
//     mutation createDeployment { ... }
//     mutation updateDeploymentStatus { ... }   // <- second write: split it out
//   }
//
// 5b. A trigger on a non-automation. `@trigger` is automation-only -- a
//     logic/query/mutation carrying one is rejected (see `_logic.memql` §5c).
//
// See `_logic.memql` for pure decisions and `_action.memql` (I6) for the
// external-capability construct each `action(...)` step references.
// =============================================================================
